    <statement><p>The following polar equation describes a circle in rectangular coordinates:
<me>r = 12\cos\theta</me>
Locate its center on the <m>xy</m>-plane, and find the circle's radius.</p>
<p><m>(x_0,y_0)=</m> (<fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>,<fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0002"/>)</p>
<p><m>R=</m><fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>6</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>6</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>r</m>  and substituting
<m>x =r \cos \theta</m> and <m>{r}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}</m> gives
<me>{r}^{2}=12 r \cos \theta \Rightarrow {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=12 x</me>
We complete the square to obtain
<me>{x}^{2} - 12 x +{y}^{2}=0</me>
<me>\left(x - 6\right)^{2} + y^2 = {6}^{2}</me>
Thus,
<m>r=12 \cos \theta</m>  is the equation of a circle of radius
<m>6</m> and center <m>\left(6,0\right)</m>.





</solution>
